PLACES=[
N('octagon-earthworks-newark','Octagon Earthworks','Newark','oh','landmark','traces',40.051944,-82.448611,'area','public','walk','','100 BC–AD 400',
  'Part of the Newark Earthworks, built by the Hopewell culture between about 100 BC and AD 400: a great circle (the Observatory Circle, named for the mound built across its old gateway) joined to an eight-sided enclosure with eight openings, each partly blocked by a low flat-topped mound. Scholars have shown the Octagon lines up with the cycle of the moon\'s risings and settings. Moundbuilders Country Club ran a golf course over it from 1911; after a case that went to the Ohio Supreme Court, the Ohio History Connection bought out the lease in 2024. Since 2023 it has been part of the Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks World Heritage Site.',
  'Earthen walls enclosing more than 59 acres, now clear of the golf course: the circle, the Observatory Mound across its gateway, the octagon\'s walls and the small mounds in its openings. Interpretive signs stand by the parking area.',
  'A 2,000-year-old moon observatory that spent a century under a golf course.',
  'Owned by the Ohio History Connection. The Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks site said in December 2024 that from January 1, 2025 the public would have full access from dawn to dusk, 365 days a year; check ohiohistory.org for events and closures. Parking: the lot on North 33rd Street by the signs.',
  [W('Newark Earthworks'),['Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks: the Octagon opening','https://hopewellearthworks.org/honoring-ancient-indigenous-history-at-newarks-octagon-earthworks/'],
   ['WYSU: Octagon Earthworks open to the public Jan. 1 (2024)','https://wysu.org/ohio-news/2024-12-16/octagon-earthworks-open-public-january-1'],HM(272272,'Octagon Earthworks')],
  th=[40.05243,-82.44622],thn='Octagon parking, North 33rd Street',brick='no'),
N('great-circle-earthworks-heath','Great Circle Earthworks','Heath','oh','landmark','traces',40.04217,-82.43011,'site','public','walk','','100 BC–AD 400',
  'The other large surviving piece of the Newark Earthworks, a ring about 1,200 feet across, one of the largest circular earthworks in the Americas. At its centre is Eagle Mound, named by settlers for its shape. A dig in 1928 found it was not a burial mound but had been raised over a rectangular wooden building with a central hearth, taken down with ceremony first. Less than a tenth of the original 3,000-acre complex survives.',
  'The high circular wall with its ditch inside, the entrance, and Eagle Mound at the centre, reached by the inner mound trail. A museum and markers stand by the parking lots.',
  'A ring of earth a quarter-mile across with a hidden building under its central mound.',
  'Moundbuilders State Park. '+OHC+' Parking: the lots off Hebron Road (Ohio 79) and the inner mound trail.',
  [W('Newark Earthworks'),HM(206093,'Great Circle Earthworks'),HM(206095,'An Eagle at the Center')],
  th=[40.04064,-82.42873],thn='Great Circle parking, inner mound trail',brick='no'),
N('licking-county-jail-newark','Licking County Historic Jail','Newark','oh','civic','standing',40.05617,-82.40158,'exact','view','drive','','1889–1987',
  'A Richardsonian Romanesque jail by the Ohio architect J. W. Yost, opened in 1889 at a cost of $120,000. The front three floors housed the sheriff\'s and jail matron\'s families; the rear held 32 cells, men and women on separate floors, with an official capacity of 68. On July 8, 1910 a mob broke in at the north door, took out Carl Etherington, an Anti-Saloon League detective who had shot a saloon owner during raids, and hanged him from a telephone pole at the courthouse square; 58 people were indicted. Prisoners were last held here in 1987.',
  'The pink sandstone ("brownstone") pile, quarried near Millersburg, with the sheriff\'s house at the front and the cell block behind. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1889 brownstone jail, empty since 1987, where a 1910 mob dragged out a dry-law detective.',
  'County-owned, at 46 South Third Street; see it from the street. It has opened for occasional free public tours (Ohio Open Doors, September 10, 2016, per the county archives\' blog); no regular hours were found. '+TOWN,
  [HM(117853,'Licking County Sheriff\'s Residence & Jail'),HM(205988,'Licking County Historic Jail'),
   ['Licking County Records & Archives: Rare opportunity to tour historic buildings (2016)','https://lcountyrecords.blogspot.com/2016/08/rare-opportunity-to-tour-historic_25.html']],brick='no'),
N('home-building-association-bank-newark','Home Building Association Bank ("The Old Home")','Newark','oh','civic','standing',40.05784,-82.40291,'exact','open','drive','','1914–1915',
  'One of eight banks by the Chicago architect Louis Sullivan, built in 1914 and opened on August 25, 1915. On a narrow lot Sullivan went to two storeys, unusual for him. Afterwards it held another bank, a butcher shop, a jewelry store and an ice-cream parlour, each changing the inside. It was given to the Licking County Foundation in 2013; the outside was restored in 2019–2021, and the National Trust reported in 2026 that it now serves as the welcome center of Explore Licking County, the county tourism bureau.',
  'Gray-green terra cotta slabs with Sullivan\'s border ornament instead of his usual red-brown brick, a winged lion, and Sullivan\'s name in the mosaic over the door. Inside, the bank hall was kept open; the tellers\' stations were not rebuilt and their footprints are marked in the floor.',
  'A Louis Sullivan "jewel box" bank that was once a butcher shop.',
  'Given to the Licking County Foundation in 2013; at 1 North Third Street on the courthouse square. As a visitor center it is open to the public; hours were not checked. '+TOWN,
  [W('Home Building Association Bank'),['National Trust: Sullivan Building rehabilitated for modern use (2026)','https://savingplaces.org/stories/sullivan-building-jewel-box-bank-in-ohio-is-rehabilitated-for-modern-use']],brick='no'),
N('licking-county-courthouse-newark','Licking County Courthouse','Newark','oh','civic','standing',40.057778,-82.401667,'site','view','drive','','1876–1880',
  'The county\'s fourth courthouse, all of them on the public square; the first was a log building of about 1809. This one was begun in 1876 in the Second Empire style to designs by Henry E. Myer of Cleveland. A fire in 1879 destroyed its upper parts, which were rebuilt in 1880.',
  'The statues on the outside walls stand for impartial justice. The marker says the west courtroom on the second floor holds paintings, sculpture and stained glass. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working county courthouse on the square; public hours were not checked. It was opened for free tours on September 10, 2016 with the jail. '+TOWN,
  [HM(205986,'The Licking County Courthouse'),['Licking County Records & Archives: Rare opportunity to tour historic buildings (2016)','https://lcountyrecords.blogspot.com/2016/08/rare-opportunity-to-tour-historic_25.html']],brick='?'),
N('buxton-inn-granville','Buxton Inn','Granville','oh','hotel','standing',40.06756,-82.51627,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1812',
  'One of Ohio\'s oldest inns, built in 1812 by Orrin Granger as "The Tavern", which was also the post office. James W. Dilley bought it in 1858 and called it the Dilley House; later owners included Major Horton Buxton and, from 1934, the opera singer Ethel "Bonnie" Bounell. Orville and Audrey Orr bought it in 1972 and restored it. Local lore says the cellar had an Underground Railroad tunnel and that the inn is haunted; neither is settled.',
  'An L-shaped two-storey main house painted red, with white columns along a two-storey porch, round a courtyard; four more houses hold guest rooms. The main-house floor is uneven with two centuries of wear.',
  'Ohio\'s 1812 tavern, still taking guests.',
  'A privately owned working inn and restaurant at 313 East Broadway; the public rooms are open as a business, hours not checked. '+TOWN,
  [W('Buxton Inn')],brick='some'),
N('avery-downer-house-granville','Avery–Downer House (Robbins Hunter Museum)','Granville','oh','landmark','standing',40.06765,-82.51761,'exact','open','drive','','1842',
  'A Greek Revival house built for Alfred Avery in 1842 and enlarged in 1873. It was a fraternity house until 1956, when the antiquarian Robbins Hunter Jr. bought it, filled it with antiques and added a Victorian clock tower and an octagon room. The Licking County Historical Society owns it and runs it as the Robbins Hunter Museum.',
  'The temple-fronted house and gardens, with Hunter\'s antiques inside.',
  '',
  'At 221 East Broadway. The museum\'s page (read November 2026) gives the season as May 8 to December 19, open Wednesday, Friday and Saturday 1–4 p.m. with guided tours, Thursday by appointment; admission free for general visitors. '+TOWN,
  [W('Avery–Hunter House'),['Robbins Hunter Museum: visit','https://www.robbinshunter.org/visit-us'],HM(205976,'Robbins Hunter Museum')],brick='?'),
N('st-lukes-episcopal-church-granville','St. Luke\'s Episcopal Church','Granville','oh','worship','standing',40.0675,-82.519722,'exact','view','drive','','1837',
  'Built in 1837 and consecrated in 1838 for a congregation formed in 1827 out of a split in the town\'s Congregational church. The exterior was designed by Benjamin Morgan (with Minard Lafever, the marker says) for Alfred Avery; Orren Bryant, a farmer from Alexandria, did the plastering. Its 1976 Register nomination called it one of the finest Greek Revival buildings in the country, in its original form.',
  'A temple-form church, 43 by 65 feet, on a base of local sandstone, with wood siding shaped to look like stone blocks and 18-foot Doric columns at the entrance. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active Episcopal parish at 111 East Broadway; see it from the street, or go in at services. '+TOWN,
  [W('St. Luke\'s Episcopal Church (Granville, Ohio)'),HM(163051,'St. Luke\'s Episcopal Church')],brick='no'),
N('old-colony-burying-ground-granville','Old Colony Burying Ground','Granville','oh','burial','standing',40.0656,-82.52003,'site','view','walk','cemeteries','1806–1935',
  'Laid out on the town plan by the Licking Company settlers from Granville, Massachusetts, before they left New England in 1805. The first burial was in 1806 and the oldest surviving stone is dated 1808; there were more than 2,000 burials by the mid-1800s and the last in 1935. Veterans of the Revolution, the War of 1812, the Mexican War and the Civil War lie here.',
  'Rows of gravestones facing west: early ones of local sandstone, later ones of marble brought up the Granville Feeder of the Ohio and Erie Canal, many signed by local carvers such as Thomas and Rollin Hughes and Manley Whipple.',
  'The pioneer graveyard a New England colony planned before it left home.',
  'On South Main Street (Ohio 661) at West Maple Street, with markers inside the north-west corner. Ownership and hours were not checked. '+TOWN,
  [HM(757,'Old Colony Burying Ground, 1805'),HM(211501,'Old Colony Burying Ground')],brick='no'),
N('bryn-du-mansion-granville','Bryn Du Mansion','Granville','oh','landmark','standing',40.06748,-82.49021,'exact','view','drive','','1865',
  'Built in 1865 by Henry D. Wright as an Italianate villa of sandstone quarried on the property; Jonas McCune owned it within a year ("McCune\'s Villa"). The coal and railroad man John Sutphin Jones bought it in 1905 and had the Columbus architect Frank Packard turn it into a Georgian-Federal mansion over five years. Later a restaurant, a company headquarters and Dave Longaberger\'s, it was bought by the Village of Granville in 2002 after a vote.',
  'The 52-room mansion behind a 32-acre front lawn, with a 1920s Mission-style carriage house, a horse barn and gardens. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Village-owned, run by the Bryn Du Commission, 537 Jones Road; it hosts private hire and public events (see bryndu.org for the event list). No open hours for walk-in visitors were found. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Bryn Du Mansion'),['Bryn Du Mansion','https://www.bryndu.org/']],brick='no'),
N('alligator-effigy-mound-granville','Alligator Effigy Mound','Granville','oh','landmark','traces',40.06993,-82.50103,'site','view','drive','','AD 800–1200',
  'An earthen figure of a four-legged animal with a long curling tail on a bluff above Granville, about 250 feet long and four feet high, one of only two effigy mounds known in Ohio with Serpent Mound. It was not a burial mound. Squier and Davis surveyed it for the Smithsonian in the 1840s. Its builders are not settled: Wikipedia gives the Fort Ancient culture, about AD 800 to 1200; the 1998 state marker says it may be Hopewell work.',
  'The low animal-shaped mound on the green inside the loop of Bryn du Drive, ringed by houses; it shows on the bare-ground LiDAR. The state marker is beside it.',
  'Ohio\'s other effigy mound, more panther than alligator.',
  'On privately owned land that Wikipedia says is open to the public. The marker is on Bryn du Drive, about a mile off Newark-Granville Road, the only road in (a dead end, not a loop). Keep to the public way; parking not checked.',
  [W('Alligator Effigy Mound'),HM(17482,'"Alligator" Mound')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('flint-ridge-ancient-quarries','Flint Ridge Ancient Quarries','Glenford','oh','industry','traces',39.98794,-82.26150,'site','public','walk','','',
  'For more than 10,000 years Flint Ridge was one of the most important flint quarries in eastern North America. People dug hundreds of pits along the ridge for the bright, many-coloured flint, which the Hopewell especially prized and traded as far as Kansas City and the Gulf of Mexico. In later times local industry cut the flint for grindstones. Ohio flint is the state gemstone.',
  'Old quarry pits and workshops along the trails, and the Gilbert W. Dilley Museum on the grounds. On the bare-ground LiDAR the woods round the pin are pocked with hundreds of pits.',
  'The prehistoric quarry that armed half a continent.',
  'Flint Ridge Ancient Quarries & Nature Preserve, 7091 Brownsville Road, at Flint Ridge Road. '+OHC,
  [W('Flint Ridge State Memorial'),HM(12949,'Flint Ridge'),HM(12950,'The Gilbert W. Dilley Museum at Flint Ridge')],
  brick='no'),
N('blackhand-gorge-canal-lock','Blackhand Gorge canal locks and towpath','Toboso','oh','canal','ruins',40.05459,-82.22470,'area','public','walk','canal','1828–1913',
  'The Ohio and Erie Canal came through the gorge in 1828: an inlet lock let boats into the Licking River, a dam at Toboso held a pool through the gorge, and an outlet lock let them out again, with a stone towpath built where the cliffs were blasted back. The blasting destroyed the black hand petroglyph the gorge is named for. The 1913 flood ended navigation. The Central Ohio Railroad cut the 64-foot-deep "Deep Cut" through the rock in 1850–51, and the interurban blasted a 327-foot tunnel on the far bank in 1903; it ran until February 15, 1929.',
  'Ohio DNR says sections of the towpath and the canal locks can be seen from the trails by the river; the Canal Lock Trail (0.4 mile) leads to them. The paved Blackhand Trail follows the old rail line. Where exactly the lock walls are was not seen; the pin is on the Canal Lock Trail.',
  'Canal, railroad and interurban squeezed one after another through a sandstone gorge.',
  'Blackhand Gorge State Nature Preserve, Ohio DNR. Open from half an hour before sunrise to half an hour after sunset. Stay on the trails; pets only on the paved Blackhand Trail, on a leash of 6 feet or less; bicycles only on that trail. OpenStreetMap maps two lots at Toboso, at the east end, in the Dillon Wildlife Area and Recreation Area; which one serves the trails was not checked, so none is given here. The federal land map marks the preserve closed (XA), but the DNR page describes it as open on its trails.',
  [W('Blackhand Gorge State Nature Preserve'),['Ohio DNR: Blackhand Gorge State Nature Preserve','https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/blackhand-gorge-state-nature-preserve']],
  brick='?'),
N('hebron-mill','Hebron Mill','Hebron','oh','industry','standing',39.96208,-82.48874,'exact','view','drive','canal','1880',
  'Built in 1880 by William Bebout where the National Road crossed the Ohio and Erie Canal, on the edge of the turning basin where canal boats loaded and turned round; it replaced a mill lost to fire in 1881 by one marker\'s account (the two markers differ on the dates). It ground flour and sawed timber, changed from water power to a 35-horsepower engine by 1891, and ran into the 1990s, Hebron\'s longest-running business. A design firm bought it in 2003 and made it its offices.',
  'The mill building at Main Street (U.S. 40) and Basin Street, with two markers. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private offices at 149 East Main Street; see it from the street. '+TOWN,
  [HM(13879,'Hebron Milling Company'),HM(97543,'Hebron Mill')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('buckeye-lake-park-fountain','Buckeye Lake Park fountain','Buckeye Lake','oh','amuse','traces',39.93267,-82.47838,'site','public','drive','trolleyparks,amusements','1904–1960s',
  'The canal\'s Licking Summit Reservoir, dammed in the 1820s, became the public park Buckeye Lake in 1894. The Columbus, Buckeye Lake and Newark Traction Company built an amusement park at the end of its interurban line here in 1904; by the 1940s it drew up to 50,000 a day to the "Dips" roller coaster, the Crystal Pool and the ballrooms where Glenn Miller and Tommy Dorsey played. The park declined in the 1960s and the state took the ground.',
  'The fountain that was the centre of the midway, the only piece of the amusement park left, beside the state marker in the state park lot.',
  'A trolley park reduced to one fountain in a boat-trailer lot.',
  'Buckeye Lake State Park (Ohio DNR), the North Shore lot off Walnut Road (Ohio 79). General park hours were not found on the DNR page; beaches are open Memorial Day to Labor Day.',
  [HM(12960,'Buckeye Lake Park'),HM(183074,'Buckeye Lake Amusement Park'),['Ohio DNR: Buckeye Lake State Park','https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/buckeye-lake-state-park']],
  th=[39.93296,-82.47865],thn='North Shore lot, Buckeye Lake State Park',brick='?'),
N('outville-depot','Outville Depot','Outville','oh','rail','standing',39.994722,-82.596944,'area','view','drive','stations','1899',
  'The Central Ohio Railroad reached here in 1853 and called the stop Kirkersville Station; stationmaster James Outcalt renamed the town after himself. The Baltimore and Ohio built this depot in 1899. It closed after 1940 and was sold and moved away in 1963; the Harrison Township trustees brought it back in 1993. The marker calls it the only depot of its type left on the line and one of a handful of original railroad buildings between Columbus and Pittsburgh.',
  'A Queen Anne and Stick-style country depot. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'In the Harrison Township Community Complex off Outville Road, just south of the tracks (the railroad is live: stay off it). Parking not checked.',
  [HM(16712,'Outville')],brick='?'),
N('ohio-canal-groundbreaking-heath','Ohio Canal groundbreaking site','Heath','oh','canal','marker',40.01429,-82.456242,'site','view','drive','canal','1825',
  'On July 4, 1825 Governor DeWitt Clinton of New York and Governor Jeremiah Morrow of Ohio turned the first earth for the Ohio and Erie Canal at the Licking Summit here. The canal was to carry Ohio\'s farm produce to Lake Erie and the Ohio River. The young James A. Garfield led tow horses on it in the early 1840s.',
  'A state marker on Hebron Road. What is left of the canal at this spot was not checked.',
  '',
  'The marker is on Hebron Road (Ohio 79) near Coffman Boulevard; the National Register site is about 200 m to the south-west. Parking not checked.',
  [HM(2377,'Ohio Canal Ground-Breaking'),W('Blackhand Gorge State Nature Preserve')],brick='no'),
N('licking-summit-deep-cut-millersport','Deep Cut at the Licking Summit','Millersport','oh','canal','traces',39.89962,-82.53494,'area','view','drive','canal','1825–1831',
  'South of the Licking Reservoir (Buckeye Lake) the canal engineers met a ridge, and since the reservoir could not be raised, they cut the ridge down to its level. The Deep Cut, 32 feet at its deepest and nearly two miles long south from Millersport, was dug mainly by Irish labourers with picks, shovels, wheelbarrows and ox carts; the first boat went through in 1831. Canal stones from Buckeye Lake are set up as a marker at the Lions park nearby.',
  'The marker in Millersport. Whether the cut can still be seen, and from where, was not checked: see the pin note.',
  '',
  'The marker is in Millersport, Fairfield County; the cut runs south from the village. Ownership of the cut was not checked: view from public roads. '+TOWN,
  [HM(14461,'Deep Cut at the Licking Summit'),HM(271210,'Ohio-Erie Canal Lock Stones')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
]
